  <result aggregation-status="0" type="1">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Tier 2 Results</narrative>
   </title>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" ascending="0" measure="1">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Strengthening leadership</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">A collaboration between Australian Alumni and academics from Australia and ASEAN countries has yielded recommendations to strengthen ties between the two regions. 
&#x200d;
Ms Lina Alexandra is an Australian Awards alumni with a Masters and PhD from the University of Queensland. She is a senior researcher at the Centre for Strategic and International Studies (CSIS), an Indonesian think tank, and is a recognised expert on ASEAN, regional security, Indonesian foreign policy, conflict prevention and peacebuilding. 
&#x200d;
Ms Alexandra co-authored the report &#x201c;Comprehensive Strategic Partners: ASEAN and Australia after the first 50 years&#x201d; with Professor Nicholas Farrelly from University of Tasmania, Ms Sharon Seah from Singapore and Dr Kimly Ngoun from Cambodia. This report, released in March 2024, commemorates 50 years of ASEAN-Australia engagement and garnered significant media attention. Notably, several recommendations of the report, including the establishment of an ASEAN-Australia Centre, have subsequently been implemented by the Australian government.</narrative>
    </description>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2024-07-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2025-06-30"></period-end>
    </period>
   </indicator>
  </result>
  <result aggregation-status="0" type="1">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Tier 2 Results</narrative>
   </title>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" ascending="0" measure="1">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Strengthening leadership</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Australia Awards Alumni play a central role in addressing Indonesia&#x2019;s development priorities, as seen in leadership teams of the National Development Planning Agency (BAPPENAS). Sixteen of 87 Echelon 1 and 2 (senior) managers have built up their qualifications and networks through Australia Awards Masters or PhD scholarships, and 27 have enhanced their knowledge on specific development priorities through Australia Awards short courses.
&#x200d;
Australia Awards recipient and a PhD alumnus from the Australian National University, Mr Pungkas Bahjuri Ali is an Expert Staff for Social Affairs and Poverty Alleviation and Head of the National Secretariat for SDGs (Sustainable Development Goals) at BAPPENAS. Mr Ali has advised the Indonesian government on key issues including improving coverage of Indonesia&#x2019;s national health insurance systems, Indonesia&#x2019;s COVID-19 response and most recently, the Makan Bergizi Gratis (Free Nutritious Meals) program. He is also fostering partnerships between BAPPENAS and the University of Sydney and Monash University to advance the SDGs in Indonesia.</narrative>
    </description>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2024-07-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2025-06-30"></period-end>
    </period>
   </indicator>
  </result>
  <result aggregation-status="0" type="1">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Tier 2 Results</narrative>
   </title>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" ascending="0" measure="1">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Strengthening leadership</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Australia Awards alumni play a vital role building cooperation between Australia and Indonesia. The personal connections they make through their time studying in Australia are the foundation of our bilateral people-to-people ties, underpinning the vast range of fields in which Australia and Indonesia cooperate to build the stability, security and prosperity of our region. Participants in the long-term and short course scholarships program make connections through their studies which enable them to establish institutional partnerships, launch their business in the Australian market, collaborate with professional counterparts, conduct joint research and attract Australian investment.</narrative>
    </description>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2023-07-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2024-06-30"></period-end>
    </period>
   </indicator>
  </result>
  <result aggregation-status="0" type="1">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Tier 2 Results</narrative>
   </title>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" ascending="0" measure="1">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Strengthening leadership</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Australia Awards in Indonesia alumni occupy prominent positions at the highest levels of government, business, civil society and creative sectors. The program targets leaders with potential to effect change in their communities and have a significant impact on the development of Indonesia, providing support pre-departure, on-award and on return. Through the substantial alumni engagement program, Australia Awards in Indonesia actively cultivates and supports the network of alumni throughout their careers, celebrating their achievements and providing a range of opportunities to ensure they maintain and strengthen their high-level connections to Australia.</narrative>
    </description>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2023-07-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2024-06-30"></period-end>
    </period>
   </indicator>
  </result>
  <result aggregation-status="0" type="1">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Tier 2 Results</narrative>
   </title>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" ascending="0" measure="1">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Strengthening leadership</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Chairperson of the Indonesian Women with Disabilities Association (HWDI) and an Australia Awards alumna Ms Revita Alvi, is championing inclusive healthcare in Gunung Kidul District in Yogyakarta. Building on her award project from the Short Course on the Role of Government and Organisations for People with Disabilities in Inclusive Development, Ms Alvi and her peers successfully piloted an accessibility assessment tool in ten community health centres. 
&#x200d;
This initiative empowered the health centres to develop an inclusive practice module, which the local government supported by committing staff training and infrastructure improvements. The project&apos;s impact resonated nationally, with a toolkit on inclusive health centres shared with BAPPENAS as a potential national standard. Expanding on this success, HWDI in collaboration with other partners launched a national social audit project assessing healthcare services in 35 community health centres across six provinces. Ms Alvi noted, &quot;we adapted the tools from the Award Project, adding budget transparency reviews.&quot; The findings, presented to national government agencies, underscored the need for enhanced training for healthcare staff on disability-inclusive care. Ms Alvi&#x2019;s work demonstrates the transformative power of inclusive initiatives in healthcare</narrative>
    </description>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2024-07-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2025-06-30"></period-end>
    </period>
   </indicator>
  </result>
